# What is a Livelit? #

# This deck follows one paper throughout: #
#   Omar, Moon, Blinn, Voysey, Collins, Chugh. #
#   Filling Typed Holes with Live GUIs. PLDI 2021. #
#   doi.org/10.1145/3453483.3454059 #
# Every "Sec." below is a section of that paper. Where this #
# implementation does something different, the slide says so, and #
# says why. The next slide, Color (Figure 3), rebuilds the paper's #
# own example line by line. #

# A livelit is a live GUI that fills a typed hole in a program #
# (Sec. 1, Sec. 2). You write the GUI once; every USE of it is then a #
# widget sitting in the code, right where the value goes. #

# The widget is not a preview. Its state -- the MODEL -- is kept in #
# the program itself (Sec. 3.2.1), so each use remembers what you #
# did to it. A use is given its meaning by expansion (Sec. 2.2): the #
# model and its splices EXPAND to what the use means. Only the model #
# is saved; expand rebuilds the rest when needed (Sec. 3.2.5). #

# The paper writes a livelit as $color. Here the name has a leading #
# caret instead, ^color, and it is bound to a module. #

# THE SIGNATURE #

# The paper splits a livelit into a declaration -- its name and #
# expansion type (Sec. 3.1) -- and an implementation (Sec. 3.2). #
# Here they are one module, checked against this builtin signature: #

#   type Livelit = {                                      #
#     type Model;          the state of one use           #
#     type Action;         what the GUI emits             #
#     type Expansion;      what a use means               #
#     let init   : UpdateCmd(Model);                      #
#     let update : Model -> Action -> UpdateCmd(Model);   #
#     let view   : Model -> ViewCmd(Html.T);              #
#     let expand :                                        #
#       + Functional( Model -> Expansion )                #
#       + Macro( Model -> (Exp, List(SpliceRef)) )        #
#   }                                                     #

# Model is Sec. 3.2.1, Action Sec. 3.2.2, view Sec. 3.2.3, update #
# Sec. 3.2.4, expand Sec. 3.2.5. Expansion is the one clients see: a #
# use has that type however expand is written (Sec. 2.3), so a #
# client can reason about a use without reading the widget or its #
# expansion (Sec. 1.2, Sec. 2.2). #

# The Functional arm is an addition here. The paper's expand is the #
# Macro arm alone: quoted code over the splices, plus the splice #
# list (Sec. 3.2.5). Functional lets a livelit whose use means a #
# plain value compute that value instead of writing a program. #

# COMMANDS #

# The members that act are COMMANDS, not plain functions. view #
# does not return Html; it returns a ViewCmd that yields Html. update #
# does not return the next model; it returns an UpdateCmd that yields #
# it. That is the paper's design (Sec. 3.2.3, Sec. 3.2.4), and it is #
# what lets a livelit touch splices -- next section. init is an #
# UpdateCmd as well, as in the paper (Sec. 3.2.1). It is performed #
# when a use is created, by typing ^name then a space, and the new #
# use's text is the model it answers, with the splices it made. #

# Two ways to write a command: #

#   Pure(x)              answers x, and does nothing else #
#   do p <- c in body    runs command c, binds its answer to the #
#                        pattern p, then runs the command body #

# Every livelit in this deck answers with Pure. One with commands to #
# sequence runs them with do, then answers with Pure, where the #
# paper's do block ends in return (Fig. 3 l.13). The do form is the #
# paper's own notation: Sec. 3 says Figure 3 is written with #
# do-notation for presentation. Here it is real syntax. A bind is #
# not recursive -- p does not reach into c -- so a recursive helper #
# goes in an ordinary let between two binds. #

# There are two command types, and the difference between them is #
# the point (Sec. 3.2.4). An UpdateCmd may make and write splices. A #
# ViewCmd may read and show them. An UpdateCmd may NOT evaluate a #
# splice, so that the model cannot depend directly on which closure #
# the reader has selected (a view can still put a result into an #
# Action it emits, Sec. 3.2.4). A do block is in one of the two, so #
# a command of the other kind -- eval_splice inside update, say -- #
# is a type error at that command. #

# SPLICES #

# A splice is a region of the CLIENT's program that sits inside the #
# widget: an editor for ordinary code (Sec. 2.4.2), in the client's #
# scope (Sec. 2.4.3). The livelit holds a SpliceRef, a handle to #
# one: just SpliceRef, never SpliceRef(Int), as in the paper, where #
# the splice has a type and the handle does not (Sec. 3.2.1). Here a #
# splice is typed only by where it sits. Under a bare SpliceRef, as #
# in Color (Figure 3), that is ?, so no type is checked there yet. #

# Five commands, with the paper's names: #

#   new_splice  : (Typ, Maybe(Exp)) -> UpdateCmd(SpliceRef)  3.2.1 #
#   set_splice  : (SpliceRef, Exp)  -> UpdateCmd(())         3.2.4 #
#   eval_splice : SpliceRef -> ViewCmd(Maybe(Result))        3.2.3 #
#   editor      : (SpliceRef, Dim)  -> ViewCmd(Html.T)       3.2.3 #
#   result_view : (SpliceRef, Dim)  -> ViewCmd(Maybe(Html.T)) 3.2.3 #

# eval_splice answers Some(Val(v)) or Some(Indet); None, for no run #
# at all, cannot happen while a ref carries its value. The paper's #
# Val and Indet each hold an Exp, such as IntLit (Sec. 3.2.3); here #
# Val holds the value itself, and Indet holds nothing. #

# The splice store is the program text. When a model is committed, #
# each SpliceRef in it is written back as the splice itself, in #
# parens, where the ref sits in the use's model argument. Each pass #
# reads those parens back as refs, directed by the Model type. So #
# parens in a use's text are how its splices are saved, not a way to #
# make one: new_splice is the only command that makes a splice #
# (Sec. 3.2.1). #

# Splices are capture-avoiding: the expansion takes them as function #
# arguments, so a splice sees the client's bindings and never the #
# livelit's (Sec. 2.4.3). The paper lists no command to delete one. #
# Here deletion is implicit: a splice that no ref in the committed #
# model reaches is not written back, so it is gone. #

# WHAT DOES NOT RUN YET #

# Two departures are flagged above: Functional is an addition, and #
# a Val holds a value, not an Exp. Beyond those, four parts of the #
# paper's design are not here yet: #

# result_view does not run yet. It is in the types, and a livelit #
# that uses it typechecks, but running it reports #
# "not implemented". The other four splice commands run, and #
# Color (Figure 3) uses all four. Pure and the do form run too: the #
# runner performs a bind by running its command and passing the #
# answer on. #

# There is no quotation yet. The paper writes Exp and Typ values #
# with quasiquotation, like `0` (Sec. 3.2.1), and Macro returns a #
# quoted function over the splices (Sec. 3.2.5). Here Exp has one #
# constructor, IntLit(Int), which Fig. 3 l.49-52 writes. So #
# new_splice can start a splice only empty or at a number, and #
# set_splice can only give it a number. A Macro expand cannot return #
# real code: its Exp can only be a hole or an IntLit, and a use does #
# not call it yet, so a use of a Macro livelit means a hole of its #
# Expansion type. Typ is a stopgap, a closed list of base type names #
# like IntT. new_splice takes one and discards it: a splice is typed #
# only by where it sits. #

# SpliceRef is not abstract yet. A ref carries the value its code #
# had in the current run, and its constructor is visible, so an #
# update could take the value out of a ref, which Sec. 3.2.4 is #
# designed to prevent. #

# The paper's implementation also lists a context: the bindings from #
# its definition site that the expansion may use (Sec. 3.2, Fig. 3 #
# l.6), which keeps an expansion independent of where it is used #
# (Sec. 3.2.5). There is no such member here. #

# Two more differences come from the language, not the paper. Hazel #
# cannot write an applied type, so you cannot yourself annotate #
# something as UpdateCmd(Model). The member's expected type supplies #
# it, which is also why Pure needs no declaration. And the paper's #
# Html takes as a parameter the Action its handlers emit, so view #
# gives ViewCmd(Html(Action)) (Sec. 3.2.3). Html.T takes none here: #
# Hazel has no type-level application to give it one. #

# A LIVELIT IN FULL #

# The smallest thing that is still a livelit: a toggle whose #
# expansion is a Bool. No splices, so every command it runs is Pure. #

let ^flag = {
  type Model = Bool;
  type Action = Bool;
  type Expansion = Bool;

  # init is a command: typing ^flag then a space performs it, and #
  # the model it answers, false, is the new use's text. #
  let init = Pure(false);

  # update takes the model, then the action, and returns a COMMAND. #
  # Pure(a) is the command that answers a -- the next model -- and #
  # does nothing else. #
  let update = fun _m : Model -> fun a : Action -> Pure(a);

  # view returns a command too. Handlers carry Actions, not #
  # functions: on_click(...) says which Action a click emits. #
  let view = fun m : Model -> Pure(
    Html.button(
      [Attr.on_click(if m then false else true),
       Attr.style([("font-family", "var(--code-font)"),
                   ("font-size", "0.8em")])],
      [Html.text(if m then "yes" else "no")]));

  # what a use means: here, the model itself, so Functional. Macro, #
  # the paper's arm, cannot return real code yet: no quotation. #
  let expand = Functional(fun m : Model -> m);

  # optional: the widget's footprint, in character cells #
  let shape = Inline(5)
} in

# Each use carries its own model, right here in the text: click one #
# and watch its argument change, and the result with it. The two #
# toggles are independent -- one use knows nothing of the other. #

(^^livelit(^flag(true)), ^^livelit(^flag(false)))

# WHAT IS CHECKED, AND WHERE #

# At the DEFINITION: the module is checked against Livelit. update #
# must take a Model then an Action and give back an UpdateCmd(Model); #
# view must give back a ViewCmd(Html.T); expand must be one arm of #
# the sum. A wrong member reads as an ordinary type error at that #
# member. #

# At each USE: in the paper this is the ONLY check that what expand #
# generates has the declared type (Sec. 3.2.5). expand returns #
# untyped quoted code, and the type of the function it quotes #
# depends on every splice in the list, which grows and shrinks per #
# use. So nothing ahead of time says that the generated code has the #
# declared Expansion type. Checking expand at the definition is an #
# addition here, and is enough for Functional. The use-site check #
# runs at every use today. For a Functional livelit it repeats what #
# the definition already guaranteed. For Macro it checks only a #
# placeholder until there is quotation; then it does real work. #

